  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Amazon Web Services has $570M in contract obligations for FY2026 to date. Amazon Web Services has 119 awards this fiscal year to date and an average award size of $4.8M. Amazon Web Services obligations are up 81.4% year over year. Amazon Web Services obligations increased across the last three reported fiscal years: FY23 at $98.6M, FY24 at $246M, FY25 at $314M, and FY26 at $570M. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $376M, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $103M and the General Services Administration at $33.5M. Computing Infrastructure Providers, Data Processing, Web Hosting, and Related Services is the largest NAICS market for Amazon Web Services with $545M in obligations. Amazon Web Services also has obligations in Computer Systems Design Services at $23.7M and Custom Computer Programming Services at $2.1M. The FY2026 totals are fiscal year to date and represent partial-year obligations. The largest buying agencies and markets reflect where federal cloud and data services spending is concentrated this fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Amazon Web Services's federal contracting trended in recent years?",
        "body": "Amazon Web Services obligations rose across the latest fiscal years reported: FY23 at $98.6M, FY24 at $246M, FY25 at $314M, and FY26 at $570M. Amazon Web Services obligations are up 81.4% year over year for FY2026 to date, reflecting larger and more numerous awards this fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main federal buyers of Amazon Web Services?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $376M to Amazon Web Services in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $103M, the General Services Administration obligated $33.5M, and the Department of Justice obligated $25.6M, with other agencies contributing smaller obligations."
      }
    ]
